  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 313 AM EDT Monday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1: After a mostly dry but relatively dreary day today, the \nnext round of rain will make its way into the region for tonight \nthrough tomorrow. A surface low will pass through from west to east, \nfollowing a stationary boundary that will be in place ahead of time. \nWhile the low will be quick moving and technically more northern \nstream and not have a strong connection to Gulf/Atlantic moisture, \nit will include the remnants of Tropical Storm Rachael so there will \nbe some efficient rainfall with the former tropical moisture. HREF \nprobabilities of half an inch are between 70-100 percent for \nnorthern areas, dropping to between 50-70 for southern areas. \nProbabilities of an inch are between 50-80 percent for northern \nareas, dropping to the 10-40 percent range for southern areas. \nOverall, it will be a beneficial soaking rain. South of the front, \nmeager instability will be able to develop. Combined with strong \ndynamics from the low, a couple stronger storms are possible, \nespecially over southern St. Lawrence County. The exact northern \nextent will depend on the specific low track. Warm air aloft will \nremain on the backside so even though the cold front drops \ntemperatures significantly, any upslope showers look to remain rain \nin the mountains. Conditions trend drier for Wednesday, but under a \nclassic cyclonic northwest flow cold air advection regime, there \nwill be some upslope sprinkles and showers but no widespread rain. \nThursday looks dry, seasonably cool and should welcome the return of \nthe sun for a bit.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2: An upper level trough and surface low pressure system \nwill pass through the St Lawrence valley on Friday. Widespread rain \nis expected with this system as well as breezy conditions. \nWidespread rainfall amounts between 0.5 and one inch appear likely \nbased on latest NWP consensus, and PoPs for late Friday are in the \n70-90% range. After the trailing cold front sweeps through, the \ntemperatures will be much cooler through the weekend. The 12Z GFS \ndepicts a particularly deep trough, consistent with previous runs. \nThe 850mb temperatures on the GFS drop to -7C to -8C by 18Z \nSaturday, and then down to -8C to -9C by 18Z Sunday. While it is \nworth noting that the GFS tends to have a cold bias in these setups, \nthe current forecast confidently reflects the potential for below-\nnormal temperatures based on the broader 12Z NWP model suite. Expect \nhighs generally in the mid-upper 40s for both Saturday and Sunday, \nquite chilly. With a northwesterly flow and sufficient moisture in \nplace, we cannot rule out upslope/mountain snow showers or flurries \nover the weekend, with some minor summit-level accumulations \npossible.",
